# Names of the backend's environment variables, with no values (T003 of specs/001-self-hosted-club-site).
# Copy to .env, which is git-ignored, and fill it in on the machine that runs the server. Never put a real value in
# this file: it is committed. Node reads .env with --env-file (Node 20.6 and later); a variable already set in the
# real environment wins over the file.
#
# The server reads exactly these names (server/src/config.ts, task T008) and fails at start when a required one is
# missing. A name here is added in the same change as the code that reads it.

# --- Phase 0: skeleton ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Port the Express app listens on inside its container or on this machine.
PORT=
# Full PostgreSQL connection string for the application's own role.
DATABASE_URL=
# Password of the PostgreSQL superuser role that the development compose file creates the database with.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
# The site's own origin (scheme, host, port) that the cross-site request guard compares the Origin header with.
# Several origins may be listed, comma separated, because a browser treats localhost and 127.0.0.1 as two different
# origins (changed 2026-10-11). On a development machine the port is the PORT above, for example
#   SITE_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000,http://127.0.0.1:3000
# In production it is the one public address, https://<the club's domain>, with no path and no trailing slash. The
# first entry is the canonical one. At start the server warns when a loopback origin names another port than PORT.
SITE_ORIGIN=

# Where the site's pages reach the backend (src/lib/api-client.ts, T031). Optional: left unset, the loopback address of
# PORT is used, which is right whenever Astro runs inside the backend's own process.
# BACKEND_ORIGIN=

# The superuser setup door (T169, added 2026-10-11): for a hosting with no command line. While no account holds the
# role, the page /auth/setup/ makes the first superuser from a code. SETUP_CODE is that code, chosen by the owner:
# at least 24 characters, long and random; remove it after the superuser exists (the page is shut for good by then).
# Left unset, the server makes a code itself and prints it in its log at start, renewed every hour while the door is
# open. ALLOW_SUPERUSER_SETUP=1 is the emergency switch: it opens the same page although a superuser exists, and the
# new account takes the role (the old holder stays an admin); set it, restart, use it, and remove it again.
# Changed 2026-10-11 (T170): the switch works only together with SETUP_CODE (with no SETUP_CODE it is ignored and the
# log says so), for 30 minutes after the server starts, and for one use. SUPERUSER.md has the whole picture.
# SETUP_CODE=
# ALLOW_SUPERUSER_SETUP=

# --- Phase 4: video (added 2026-10-11, T079) ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# The folder that holds the video files: a mounted volume, never inside the image and never the database's volume.
# `npm run video:register` registers an MP4 copied into it; the stream route (T080) serves from it.
VIDEO_ROOT=
# Behind nginx (production): the internal location that serves the files, for the X-Accel-Redirect answer of
# /media/video/:slug (T080), for example VIDEO_ACCEL=/_video/ . Left unset (development), the server sends the file
# and the ranges itself.
# VIDEO_ACCEL=

# --- Phase 6: uploads from the panel (added 2026-10-12, T098) -----------------------------------------------------
# The largest video file the panel accepts in one upload, in MiB. Optional: left empty it is 500. The server refuses a
# larger file from the request's Content-Length before it reads a byte, and stops at the start on a value that is not
# a whole number from 1 to 100000. Behind nginx the proxy has a limit of its own, `client_max_body_size`, which must be
# at least this large for the upload route (the nginx default is 1 MiB); it lives in nginx's configuration, not here.
# A change here needs a restart of the application.
# VIDEO_UPLOAD_CAP_MB=
# The room that must stay free on the video volume after an upload, in MiB (T138, 2026-10-12). Optional: left empty it
# is 2048 (2 GiB); 0 switches the reserve off. An upload that would leave less is refused before a byte is stored, so
# the database, its backups and the system keep room to work in. The database's data and its backups must not share the
# volume with the video files, or this rule protects nothing. A change here needs a restart.
# VIDEO_FREE_RESERVE_MB=
# The size cap of one picture an editor uploads for a post, in MiB (T143, 2026-10-12). Optional: left empty it is 5; a
# whole number from 1 to 100. The pictures live on the same volume under images/ and obey the same free-space reserve.
# The proxy's `client_max_body_size` must be at least this large as well. A change here needs a restart.
# IMAGE_UPLOAD_CAP_MB=

# --- Phase 7: feedback and mail (added when the phase starts) -----------------------------------------------------
# The mailbox the server sends from (T108, 2026-10-13). Set all four, or none: a half-written set stops the start with
# the names that are missing. With none set the queue of outgoing mail simply waits and nothing is lost; the start line
# of the server says which it is. The password is the mailbox's own app password, never the account's main one.
# SMTP_HOST is a host name only, with no port. Port 465 is encrypted from the first byte; any other port must offer
# STARTTLS and the send fails, with the reason in the admin list, when it does not (587 is the usual one).
# SMTP_HOST=
# SMTP_PORT=
# SMTP_USER=
# SMTP_PASSWORD=
# The sender's address, when SMTP_USER is a login name and not an address. One bare address, no display name.
# SMTP_FROM=
# Added 2026-10-13 (T111): `npm run mail:check` connects with these values exactly as the server does, says which step
# fails and why, and with `-- --to <address>` sends one test message. Run it on the server itself; MAIL.md has the rest.
# Development only: when no SMTP variable is set, each outgoing message is written as a text file into this folder
# instead of being sent, so the notice of the feedback form can be read on a machine with no mail account. Files are
# only created, never overwritten or removed. A real SMTP configuration always wins over it.
# MAIL_OUTBOX_DIR=
# Seeds the feedback recipient on the first start only; afterwards the setting lives in the settings table and is
# changed from the superuser's page. The address itself is never written into the repository.
# Read at every start but used only when no recipient is set (T110, 2026-10-13); a value that is not one bare
# address stops the start with this name.
# FEEDBACK_RECIPIENT_SEED=
